### Description

The Terraform program below creates an AWS Elastic Beanstalk application and configures an application version lifecycle policy to retain versions for 5 days. The program includes necessary IAM roles and policies to ensure proper permissions for Elastic Beanstalk operations. It also sets up a valid AWS provider configuration within the `us-west-2` region.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

variable "application_name" {
  description = "The name of the Elastic Beanstalk application"
  default     = "MyElasticBeanstalkApp"
}

variable "environment_name" {
  description = "The name of the Elastic Beanstalk environment"
  default     = "MyElasticBeanstalkEnv"
}

variable "solution_stack_name" {
  description = "The solution stack name for the Elastic Beanstalk environment"
  default     = "64bit Amazon Linux 2 v3.6.0 running Python 3.9"
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "elastic_beanstalk_service_role" {
  name = "${var.application_name}-service-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "elasticbeanstalk.amazonaws.com"
        }
      },
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "elastic_beanstalk_service_policy" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.elastic_beanstalk_service_role.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSElasticBeanstalkWebTier"
}

resource "aws_elastic_beanstalk_application" "my_app" {
  name        = var.application_name
  description = "My Elastic Beanstalk Application"
}

resource "aws_elastic_beanstalk_application_version" "my_app_version" {
  application = aws_elastic_beanstalk_application.my_app.name
  bucket      = "my-s3-bucket"
  key         = "my-app-version.zip"
  version_label = "v1.0.0"
}

resource "aws_elastic_beanstalk_environment" "my_env" {
  name                = var.environment_name
  application         = aws_elastic_beanstalk_application.my_app.name
  solution_stack_name = var.solution_stack_name
  version_label       = aws_elastic_beanstalk_application_version.my_app_version.version_label
  service_role        = aws_iam_role.elastic_beanstalk_service_role.arn

  setting {
    namespace = "aws:elasticbeanstalk:environment"
    name      = "EnvironmentType"
    value     = "LoadBalanced"
  }
}

resource "aws_elastic_beanstalk_configuration_template" "lifecycle_template" {
  application_name = aws_elastic_beanstalk_application.my_app.name
  name             = "LifecycleTemplate"

  setting {
    namespace = "aws:elasticbeanstalk:application:versionlifecycle"
    name      = "MaxCount"
    value     = "1"
  }

  setting {
    namespace = "aws:elasticbeanstalk:application:versionlifecycle"
    name      = "MaxAgeInDays"
    value     = "5"
  }
}

resource "aws_elastic_beanstalk_application_version_lifecycle_config" "lifecycle_config" {
  application_name = aws_elastic_beanstalk_application.my_app.name
  template_name    = aws_elastic_beanstalk_configuration_template.lifecycle_template.name
}
```

### Explanation

1. **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured to use the `us-west-2` region.
2. **Variables**: Variables are defined for the application name, environment name, and solution stack name with default values.
3. **IAM Role**: An IAM role is created for the Elastic Beanstalk service with the necessary permissions attached.
4. **Elastic Beanstalk Application**: An Elastic Beanstalk application is created.
5. **Application Version**: An application version is created, pointing to an S3 bucket and key where the application code is stored.
6. **Environment**: An Elastic Beanstalk environment is created using the application and version.
7. **Configuration Template**: A configuration template is created to define the lifecycle policy for application versions.
8. **Lifecycle Configuration**: The lifecycle configuration is applied to the application using the previously created template.

This setup ensures that the application versions are retained for 5 days, after which they will be automatically deleted.

