### Description

The Terraform script provided below creates an AWS EKS node group named "example" and links it to an existing EKS cluster also named "example". The node group is configured with specific scaling parameters and dependencies on IAM role policy attachments to ensure proper permissions management. The script includes:

- **Provider Configuration**: Sets up the AWS provider with a specified region.
- **IAM Role**: Creates an IAM role for the EKS node group with necessary policies attached.
- **EKS Node Group**: Defines the node group with attributes such as name, node role ARN, subnet IDs, and scaling parameters.
- **Scaling Parameters**: Configures the desired, maximum, and minimum sizes of the node group, along with the maximum number of unavailable nodes during updates.
- **Policy Attachments**: Ensures that the EKS worker node policy, EKS CNI policy, and EC2 container registry read-only policy are attached to the IAM role.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"  # Specify your desired AWS region
}

variable "eks_cluster_name" {
  description = "Name of the EKS cluster"
  default     = "example"
}

variable "node_group_name" {
  description = "Name of the EKS node group"
  default     = "example"
}

variable "subnet_ids" {
  description = "List of subnet IDs for the node group"
  type        = list(string)
  default     = ["subnet-0123456789abcdef0", "subnet-0fedcba9876543210"]  # Replace with your subnet IDs
}

variable "node_role_arn" {
  description = "ARN of the IAM role for the EKS node group"
  type        = string
  default     = ""  # This should be set to the ARN of the IAM role created or managed elsewhere
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "eks_node_group_role" {
  name = "${var.node_group_name}-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
      },
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "eks_worker_node_policy" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy"
  role       = aws_iam_role.eks_node_group_role.name
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "eks_cni_policy" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonEKSCNIPolicy"
  role       = aws_iam_role.eks_node_group_role.name
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "ec2_container_registry_read_only_policy" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryReadOnly"
  role       = aws_iam_role.eks_node_group_role.name
}

resource "aws_eks_node_group" "example" {
  cluster_name    = var.eks_cluster_name
  node_group_name = var.node_group_name
  node_role_arn   = var.node_role_arn
  subnet_ids      = var.subnet_ids

  scaling_config {
    desired_size = 1
    max_size     = 2
    min_size     = 1
  }

  update_config {
    max_unavailable = 1
  }

  depends_on = [
    aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.eks_worker_node_policy,
    aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.eks_cni_policy,
    aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.ec2_container_registry_read_only_policy,
  ]
}
```

### Notes

- **IAM Role ARN**: The `node_role_arn` variable should be set to the ARN of the IAM role created or managed elsewhere. If you need to create the IAM role within the same Terraform configuration, you can remove the `node_role_arn` variable and use `aws_iam_role.eks_node_group_role.arn` directly in the `aws_eks_node_group` resource.
- **Subnet IDs**: Replace the placeholder subnet IDs with your actual subnet IDs.
- **Region**: Ensure the AWS region specified in the provider configuration matches your desired deployment region.

