The Terraform program below creates a single-node Amazon Redshift cluster. It includes the necessary AWS provider configuration, IAM roles, and security groups to ensure the Redshift cluster can be properly set up and accessed. The configuration also includes default values for variables to make it deployable without additional input.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = var.region
}

variable "region" {
  description = "The AWS region to deploy the Redshift cluster"
  default     = "us-west-2"
}

variable "cluster_identifier" {
  description = "The identifier for the Redshift cluster"
  default     = "my-redshift-cluster"
}

variable "node_type" {
  description = "The type of node to use in the Redshift cluster"
  default     = "dc2.large"
}

variable "master_username" {
  description = "The username for the master user"
  default     = "admin"
}

variable "master_user_password" {
  description = "The password for the master user"
  default     = "SecurePassword123!"
}

variable "db_name" {
  description = "The name of the database to create"
  default     = "dev"
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "redshift_role" {
  name = "${var.cluster_identifier}-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "redshift.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "redshift_s3_access" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.redshift_role.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess"
}

resource "aws_security_group" "redshift_sg" {
  name        = "${var.cluster_identifier}-sg"
  description = "Security group for Redshift cluster"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id

  ingress {
    from_port   = 5439
    to_port     = 5439
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

variable "vpc_id" {
  description = "The VPC ID where the Redshift cluster will be deployed"
  default     = "vpc-12345678"
}

resource "aws_redshift_cluster" "example" {
  cluster_identifier = var.cluster_identifier
  node_type          = var.node_type
  master_username    = var.master_username
  master_user_password = var.master_user_password
  db_name            = var.db_name
  iam_roles          = [aws_iam_role.redshift_role.arn]
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.redshift_sg.id]
  publicly_accessible = true
}
```

### Explanation

1. **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured with a default region (`us-west-2`), which can be overridden using the `region` variable.

2. **Variables**: Several variables are defined with default values to customize the Redshift cluster, such as the cluster identifier, node type, master username, password, database name, and VPC ID.

3. **IAM Role**: An IAM role is created for the Redshift cluster with a trust policy allowing Redshift to assume the role. The role is attached to the `AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess` policy to allow read-only access to S3 buckets.

4. **Security Group**: A security group is created to allow inbound traffic on port 5439 (the default Redshift port) from any IP address. Outbound traffic is allowed to all destinations.

5. **Redshift Cluster**: The Redshift cluster is created with a single node, using the specified node type, master username, password, and database name. The cluster is associated with the IAM role and security group created earlier.

This configuration ensures that the Redshift cluster is properly set up with the necessary permissions and network access.

