To reduce the number of database calls, we can implement a caching mechanism. In this example, we'll use Amazon ElastiCache for Redis, which is a managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale an in-memory data store. This will help cache frequently accessed data, reducing the load on the database and improving application performance.

### Description:
The Terraform program below sets up an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster in the `us-west-2` region. It includes the necessary IAM role and policies to allow the ElastiCache service to manage the Redis cluster. The configuration also includes a security group to control access to the Redis cluster.

### Terraform HCL Program:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

variable "redis_instance_type" {
  description = "The type of Redis instance to launch"
  default     = "cache.t3.micro"
}

variable "redis_node_count" {
  description = "The number of nodes in the Redis cluster"
  default     = 1
}

variable "redis_engine_version" {
  description = "The version of the Redis engine"
  default     = "6.x"
}

variable "redis_parameter_group_name" {
  description = "The name of the parameter group to associate with the Redis cluster"
  default     = "default.redis6.x"
}

variable "redis_subnet_group_name" {
  description = "The name of the subnet group to associate with the Redis cluster"
  default     = "my-redis-subnet-group"
}

variable "vpc_id" {
  description = "The ID of the VPC where the Redis cluster will be deployed"
  default     = "vpc-12345678"
}

variable "subnet_ids" {
  description = "A list of subnet IDs where the Redis cluster will be deployed"
  type        = list(string)
  default     = ["subnet-12345678", "subnet-87654321"]
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "elasticache_role" {
  name = "ElastiCacheRole"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "ec2.amazonaws.com"
        }
      },
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "elasticache_policy" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonElastiCacheFullAccess"
  role       = aws_iam_role.elasticache_role.name
}

resource "aws_security_group" "redis_sg" {
  name        = "redis-sg"
  description = "Security group for Redis"
  vpc_id      = var.vpc_id

  ingress {
    from_port   = 6379
    to_port     = 6379
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

resource "aws_elasticache_subnet_group" "redis_subnet_group" {
  name       = var.redis_subnet_group_name
  subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids
}

resource "aws_elasticache_cluster" "redis_cluster" {
  cluster_id           = "my-redis-cluster"
  engine               = "redis"
  engine_version       = var.redis_engine_version
  node_type            = var.redis_instance_type
  num_cache_nodes      = var.redis_node_count
  parameter_group_name = var.redis_parameter_group_name
  subnet_group_name    = aws_elasticache_subnet_group.redis_subnet_group.name
  security_group_names = [aws_security_group.redis_sg.name]
  iam_role_arn         = aws_iam_role.elasticache_role.arn
}
```

### Explanation:
- **Provider Configuration**: Sets the AWS provider with the region `us-west-2`.
- **Variables**: Define variables for customizable parameters such as instance type, node count, engine version, etc.
- **IAM Role and Policy**: Creates an IAM role with the necessary permissions for ElastiCache.
- **Security Group**: Configures a security group to allow inbound traffic on port 6379 (Redis default port).
- **Subnet Group**: Defines a subnet group for the Redis cluster.
- **ElastiCache Cluster**: Creates the Redis cluster using the specified configurations.

This setup will help reduce the number of database calls by caching frequently accessed data in Redis, improving the overall performance of your application.

